id: k8s.namespace
kind: kubernetes
title: Namespace
aliases: namespace, ns
tags: operations, isolation
questions:
  - what is a namespace actually for?
invariant: A namespace scopes names, RBAC, quotas and most policy objects; it is not a network or kernel boundary.
answer_30s: Namespaces partition names and policy (RBAC, quotas, limits) inside one cluster; they are not a security boundary on their own.
answer_2m: |
  What is namespaced: names, Service DNS, RBAC role bindings, ResourceQuota,
  LimitRange, NetworkPolicy, Secrets and ConfigMaps. What is not: nodes,
  PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, CRDs, and the cluster's control plane. So
  namespaces give teams independent vocabularies and budgets, while the real
  isolation (network, kernel, node) comes from NetworkPolicy, securityContext
  and separate clusters for the highest tiers. Remember `-n` versus `-A` in
  every command you quote; a missing namespace is the most common reason "the
  object does not exist".
when_to_use:
  - team or environment separation, per-team quotas, policy scoping
commands:
  - kubectl get ns => which namespaces exist
  - kubectl get pods -A => every workload, across namespaces (the default look)
  - kubectl get resourcequota -n payments => what budget a namespace is bound to
failure_modes:
  - assuming namespaces isolate network traffic (they do not without NetworkPolicy)
  - looking for an object in the wrong namespace
related:
  - k8s.labels-selectors
  - k8s.api-server
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/namespaces/
